 in the integer variable $t$. Chapoton (2016) proved that, given a fixed integral form $\\lambda: \\mathbb{Z}^d \\to \\mathbb{Z}$, there exists a polynomial $\\text{cha}_\\mathcal{P}^\\lambda(q,x) \\in \\mathbb{Q}(q)[x]$ such that the refined enumeration function $\\sum_{ \\mathbf{m} \\in t \\mathcal{P} } q^{ \\lambda(\\mathbf{m}) }$ equals the evaluation $\\text{cha}_\\mathcal{P}^\\lambda (q, [t]_q)$ where, as usual, $[t]_q := \\frac{ q^t - 1 }{ q-1 }$; naturally, for $q=1$ we recover the Ehrhart polynomial. Our motivating goal is to view Chapoton's work through the lens of Brion's Theorem (1988), which expresses the integer-point structure of a given polytope via that of its vertex cones. It turns out that this viewpoint naturally yields various refinements and extensions of Chapoton's results, including explicit formulas for $\\text{cha}_\\mathcal{P}^\\lambda(q,x)$, its leading coefficient, and its behavior as $t \\to \\infty$. We also prove an analogue of Chapoton's structural and reciprocity theorems for rational polytopes (i.e., with vertices in $\\mathbb{Q}^d$).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Quadri-algebras, preLie algebras, and the Catalan family of Lie idempotents (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/363400592_Quadri-algebras_preLie_algebras_and_the_Catalan_family_of_Lie_idempotents)\nciteturn5search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.2 years ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; consequence of Theorem 5.1, we can express its Chapoton–Livernet basis in terms of ... In [6], Chapoton gives an expression of the Catalan ... A small closed ﬂow of size k on τ is equivalent to the following data:\nconsequence of Theorem 5.1, we can express its Chapoton–Livernet basis in terms of\n\nthe X-basis of C.\n\nLemma 5.6.For a rooted plane tree T, denote by ¯\n\nT the underlying non-plane rooted\n\ntree. The map\n\n(145) ι:p τ 7−→ |Aut(τ)|X\n\n¯\n\nT=τ\n\nX T\n\nis an embedding of preLie algebras.\n\nIn terms of the symmet